## What Terraform creates

Names use the prefix `{{ spec.name | upper }}`.

| Object | Name pattern | Example |
|--------|--------------|---------|
| Database (one per environment and layer) | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_<ENV>_<LAYER>` | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_{{ spec.environments[0] | upper }}_{{ spec.layers[0] | upper }}` |
| Schema (one per domain, in every database) | `<DOMAIN>` | `{{ spec.domains[0].name | upper }}` |
| Warehouse (one per environment, XSMALL unless set in `warehouse_sizes`) | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_<ENV>_WH` | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_{{ spec.environments[0] | upper }}_WH` |
| Read and write access role per schema | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_<ENV>_<LAYER>_<DOMAIN>_R` / `_W` | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_{{ spec.environments[0] | upper }}_{{ spec.layers[0] | upper }}_{{ spec.domains[0].name | upper }}_R` |
| Functional role per environment, from `platform.yml` | `{{ spec.name | upper }}_<ENV>_<ROLE>` | {% if spec.roles %}`{{ spec.name | upper }}_{{ spec.environments[0] | upper }}_{{ spec.roles[0].name | upper }}`{% else %}none yet{% endif %} |

Give people and services the functional roles, never the access roles. Functional roles roll up to `SYSADMIN`. A write access role includes its read role. Schemas use managed access, so only the schema owner and roles with `MANAGE GRANTS` can grant on objects in them.

## Run Terraform

Treat the account as production. Read every plan before you apply it.

1. Set credentials as environment variables (never in files): `SNOWFLAKE_ORGANIZATION_NAME`, `SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT_NAME`, `SNOWFLAKE_USER`, `SNOWFLAKE_ROLE`, and key-pair auth with `SNOWFLAKE_AUTHENTICATOR` and `SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY`. The role needs to create databases, roles, grants{% if spec.sources or spec.sso.provider == "okta" %} and integrations{% endif %}.
2. Add a backend for Terraform state (for example S3) before sharing this project. Without one, state stays on your machine.
3. Run:

    ```bash
    cd terraform
    terraform init
    terraform plan -out plan.tfplan{% if spec.sources or spec.sso.provider == "okta" %} -var-file=platform.tfvars{% endif %}
    terraform apply plan.tfplan
    ```
{%- if spec.sources or spec.sso.provider == "okta" %}

`platform.tfvars` is yours to create. It needs:
{%- if spec.sources %}
- `storage_aws_role_arn`: the IAM role Snowflake assumes to read the landing bucket.
{%- endif %}
{%- if spec.sso.provider == "okta" %}
- `okta_saml_issuer`, `okta_sso_url` and `okta_x509_cert` from the Okta Snowflake app. The certificate is sensitive; keep the file out of git or pass it with `TF_VAR_okta_x509_cert`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## After the first apply
{%- if spec.sources %}

- **S3 access.** Add the outputs `storage_iam_user_arn` and `storage_external_id` to the trust policy of the IAM role in `storage_aws_role_arn`. Snowflake cannot read the bucket until you do.
{%- endif %}
{%- if spec.network.allowed_ips %}

- **Network policy.** `{{ spec.name | upper }}_NETWORK_POLICY` is created but not attached, because attaching it blocks every other address, including other teams using this account. Once you have checked the list includes your own address, attach it as ACCOUNTADMIN: `ALTER ACCOUNT SET NETWORK_POLICY = {{ spec.name | upper }}_NETWORK_POLICY;`, or attach it to specific users.
{%- endif %}
{%- if spec.sso.provider == "okta" %}

- **Okta provisioning.** Generate a SCIM token as ACCOUNTADMIN with `SELECT SYSTEM$GENERATE_SCIM_ACCESS_TOKEN('{{ spec.name | upper }}_OKTA_SCIM');` and paste it into the Okta Snowflake app.
{%- endif %}

- **Grant functional roles to users**, or let your identity provider do it.
{%- if spec.network.allowed_ips or spec.sources %}

Provider 2.22 treats {% if spec.network.allowed_ips %}network rules{% endif %}{% if spec.network.allowed_ips and spec.sources %} and {% endif %}{% if spec.sources %}the AWS storage integration{% endif %} as preview features, so `providers.tf` turns them on. Preview features can change in minor provider releases; read the provider changelog before upgrading.
{%- endif %}

## Build with dbt

The dbt project uses dbt Core 1.12 with the Snowflake adapter:

```bash
pip install "dbt-core~=1.12" "dbt-snowflake~=1.12"
```

Set `SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT` (organization-account, such as `myorg-myaccount`), `SNOWFLAKE_USER` and `SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY`{% if not (spec.roles | selectattr("name", "equalto", "transformer") | list) %}, and `DBT_SNOWFLAKE_ROLE` (this spec has no `transformer` role, so dbt needs to be told which role to use: one that can write the model layers){% endif %}. Then:

```bash
cd dbt
dbt build --profiles-dir . --target {{ spec.environments[0] }}
```

**Where a model builds is decided by its folder.** Put each model in `models/<layer>/<domain>/`. A model in `models/{{ spec.layers[1] if spec.layers | length > 1 else spec.layers[0] }}/{{ spec.domains[0].name }}/` builds in `{{ spec.name | upper }}_<TARGET>_{{ (spec.layers[1] if spec.layers | length > 1 else spec.layers[0]) | upper }}.{{ spec.domains[0].name | upper }}`. Layers for models are {% for layer in spec.layers[1:] %}`{{ layer }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if spec.layers | length < 2 %}(none: add a layer after `{{ spec.layers[0] }}` in platform.yml){% endif %}; `{{ spec.layers[0] }}` is for loaded data only. A model anywhere else builds in the `PUBLIC` schema, which the Terraform roles cannot write to, so it fails.
{%- if spec.sources %}

Sources (`models/_sources.yml`) point at the `{{ spec.layers[0] }}` layer. The Airflow DAG creates and loads those tables; models that select from them can build once it has run.
{%- endif %}

## Run on Amazon MWAA

The DAG `{{ spec.name }}_platform` runs `{{ spec.orchestrator.schedule }}`.{% if spec.sources %} It loads every source in parallel, then runs `dbt build`.{% else %} It runs `dbt build`.{% endif %} Use one MWAA environment per platform environment, on Airflow 3.3.1.

**Upload to the environment's S3 bucket** (the bucket must block public access and have versioning on):

| From this project | To the bucket |
|-------------------|---------------|
| `airflow/dags/` | `dags/` |
| `dbt/` | `dags/dbt/` |
| `airflow/requirements.txt` | set as the environment's requirements file |
| `airflow/startup.sh` | set as the environment's startup script |

Do not upload `dbt/target/` or `dbt/logs/`.

**Set up in Airflow:**

- Variable `platform_environment`: which environment this MWAA environment serves ({{ spec.environments | join(", ") }}). Default: `{{ spec.environments[0] }}`.
{%- if spec.sources %}
- Connection `snowflake_loader` (type Snowflake, key-pair auth) for a user whose role can write the `{{ spec.layers[0] }}` layer{% if spec.roles | selectattr("name", "equalto", "loader") | list %} (`{{ spec.name | upper }}_<ENV>_LOADER`){% endif %}. Set `role` and `warehouse` in its extras.
{%- endif %}
- Connection `snowflake_dbt` (type Snowflake, key-pair auth) for the dbt user. Its extras must include `account`, `role` and `private_key_content` (the PEM text of the key).

Store the private keys in a secrets backend such as AWS Secrets Manager, not in the Airflow UI.
{%- if spec.sources %}

**How loading works.** For each source, the DAG creates a file format, a stage on `{{ spec.name | upper }}_S3_LANDING` and, on the first run, a table whose columns are inferred from the files (`INFER_SCHEMA`). It then runs `COPY INTO ... MATCH_BY_COLUMN_NAME = CASE_INSENSITIVE`. Snowflake skips files it has already loaded, so reruns do not duplicate data. CSV files must have a header row.
{%- endif %}

## Change the platform

1. Edit `platform.yml`.
2. Run `quicketl platform validate`.
3. Commit your work, then run `quicketl platform update`. It applies the new spec and any template changes, and shows conflicts with your own edits for you to resolve.

Do not edit `.copier-answers.yml` by hand.
